{
 "duration": 53.495,
 "sentences": [
  {
   "index": 0,
   "start": 0.25,
   "end": 5.841,
   "text": "Outlook and Teams just went down around the world. And Microsoft says it was not an attack."
  },
  {
   "index": 1,
   "start": 6.241,
   "end": 12.193,
   "text": "On November twelfth, Microsoft 365 started failing in more than seventy countries."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "start": 12.573,
   "end": 16.923,
   "text": "The early explanation? Not a traffic spike. Failed server health checks."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "start": 17.323,
   "end": 26.284,
   "text": "Here's what that means. A load balancer keeps asking every server: are you alive? If one stops answering, it gets pulled out of rotation."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "start": 26.663,
   "end": 32.952,
   "text": "That's perfect when one server dies. But when many servers fail their checks at once, they all get pulled."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "start": 33.332,
   "end": 39.891,
   "text": "Now the same traffic lands on far fewer machines. They overload, slow down, and start failing too."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "start": 40.251,
   "end": 42.554,
   "text": "Engineers call that a cascading failure."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "start": 42.954,
   "end": 47.223,
   "text": "Microsoft says the final root cause is still being investigated."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "start": 47.623,
   "end": 52.595,
   "text": "The lesson? Health checks decide who gets traffic. Design them to fail carefully."
  }
 ],
